 # Technical writing
@@ -72,6 +71,10 @@ Don't mix modes: no reference tables inside a tutorial, no tutorial hand-holding
 - Pick one word per action and stick to it: "start", not "start" here and "initiate" there.
 - Write procedures as direct commands, never as narration and never in the passive: "Install the component", not "the component must be installed".
 - Avoid "-ing" words where you can.
+- Strict mode for procedures, runbooks, safety text, and error messages: expand contractions, keep paragraphs to one topic and at most six sentences.
+- Define an unfamiliar term at its first use. Ground every concept before a later section relies on it: the reader either knew it coming in or met it earlier.
+- Never drop a caveat, limitation, warning, or exception to make the text shorter.
+- Use a table when the same fields repeat three or more times. Use prose when the items aren't parallel.
 
